{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Ulster and Delaware Railroad ran from Kingston Point on the Hudson River through the heart of the Catskill Mountains to Oneonta, advertised as 'The Only All-Rail Route to the Catskill Mountains.' Passenger service ran from the 1870s until March 31, 1954 (as part of the New York Central after 1932). Full mainline station order: Kingston Point/Rondout - Kingston - Shandaken - Big Indian - Fleischmanns - Arkville - Kellys Corners - Halcottsville - Roxbury - Moresville/Grand Gorge - South Gilboa - Stamford - Hobart - South Kortright - Bloomville - Kortright - East Meredith - Davenport Center - West Davenport - Oneonta. Geometry: Kingston-Phoenicia-Big Indian-Fleischmanns (partial) is real-traced from OpenStreetMap (the Kingston-Mount Tremper stretch is preserved/operated today by the Catskill Mountain Railroad); Roxbury-Grand Gorge-Stamford-Bloomville-Oneonta is likewise real-traced (this stretch is partly preserved by the Delaware & Ulster Railroad heritage line around Arkville-Roxbury). The middle stretch, roughly Fleischmanns/Arkville through Halcottsville/Kellys Corners to Roxbury (~16km), has no matching active, abandoned, or disused rail tagging in OpenStreetMap and is a straight-line approximation between the real, correctly-ordered station towns -- flagged honestly rather than guessed at closer precision. STONY CLOVE & CATSKILL MOUNTAIN BRANCH ADDED 2026-08-24: the Hunter/Tannersville narrow-gauge branch flagged as missing in this file's earlier version is now traced, as two features (both using the U&D's own company color, per this dataset's same-company-same-color convention): Phoenicia-Chichester-Lanesville-Edgewood-Hunter, and the Kaaterskill Branch from Edgewood via Stony Clove Notch to Tannersville. Organized January 1881 as an independent 3-foot narrow-gauge line, it opened to Hunter June 24 1882; the U&D acquired control of it (and the connecting Kaaterskill Railroad) June 4 1893, later standard-gauging the Kaaterskill portion in 1899. Both branches were abandoned in 1939 under New York Central ownership. Geometry is real-traced from OpenStreetMap, which tags the historic route directly as 'Hunter Branch' and 'Kaaterskill Branch'; a short stretch (~8km) between Edgewood and Stony Clove Notch has no connected tagging and is a straight-line approximation. COLOR SPLIT 2026-08-24: per the same-company-same-color rule, each feature above was split at the 1932 New York Central takeover into two features sharing identical geometry -- a U&D-colored feature for the pre-1932 segments, and an NYC-colored feature (matching new_york_central.geojson's canonical color) for 1932 onward -- rather than coloring the whole multi-era line with the U&D's own color throughout.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulster_and_Delaware_Railroad (chartered 1866 as the Rondout & Oswego; reorganized 1872 as New York, Kingston & Syracuse; renamed Ulster and Delaware 1875; at greatest extent 107 miles Kingston Point-Oneonta through Ulster, Delaware, Schoharie and Otsego counties; eastern terminal extended Rondout-Kingston Point 1895; reached Oneonta and a Delaware & Hudson connection 1900; narrow-gauge branch Phoenicia-Hunter built 1881, Kaaterskill Railway to Tannersville acquired 1892; absorbed into New York Central 1932)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gettysburg_Railroad", "search results citing full mainline station list: Shandaken, Big Indian, Grand Hotel, Fleischmanns, Arkville, Kellys Corners, Halcottsville, Roxbury, Moresville (Grand Gorge), South Gilboa, Stamford, Hobart, South Kortright, Bloomville, Kortright, East Meredith, Davenport Center, West Davenport, Oneonta; last regularly scheduled Kingston-Oneonta passenger train (New York Central) ran March 31, 1954", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catskill_Mountain_Railroad (heritage operator preserving part of the Kingston-Mount Tremper stretch)", "https://durr.org/about/ (Delaware & Ulster Railroad heritage line, Arkville-Roxbury area)", "search results and https://www.dailygazette.com/hv360/opinion/columnists/the-stony-clove-catskill-mountain-and-kaaterskill-railroads/ (Stony Clove and Catskill Mountain Railroad organized January 18 1881; open to Edgewood by August 1881; opened through to Hunter June 24 1882; U&D acquired control of it and the Kaaterskill Railroad June 4 1893; Kaaterskill Railroad converted to standard gauge 1899; competing Catskill and Tannersville Railway went bankrupt 1918, scrapped 1919)"], "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API): railway=rail (active heritage operation), railway=abandoned/disused, and named rail-trail tagging (Huckleberry Rail Trail, Catskill Scenic Trail) for the Kingston-Phoenicia-Big Indian-Fleischmanns stretch and the Roxbury-Grand Gorge-Stamford-Bloomville-Oneonta stretch; routed via Dijkstra shortest path. The Fleischmanns/Arkville-Roxbury middle stretch (~16km) has no matching geometry of any kind in OSM and is a straight-line approximation between the real station towns."], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for any era; the Hunter/Tannersville and Delhi narrow-gauge branches are not traced on this map.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Ulster and Delaware Railroad", "line": "Ulster and Delaware: Kingston/Rondout \u2013 Oneonta", "id": "ulster-delaware-railroad", "color": "#e07a5f", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 32, "speed_source": "estimated: 1870s-1954 mountain branch-line 